    "result_text": "The full test suite passes \u2014 all 3 tests green, `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (the single owner-creation endpoint).\n\n**Changes to `src/main/java/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:**\n\n1. **Added `normalizeAddress(String)`** \u2014 trims and collapses internal whitespace to a single space, upper-cases, then expands common street-type abbreviations token-by-token via a small map (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). Returns empty string for `null`/blank input.\n\n2. **Applied it in `addOwner`** right after email normalization and before any household logic:\n   - Rejects with `400 BAD_REQUEST` when the address is blank after normalization (the required-field check now operates on the normalized value).\n   - Stores the normalized address on the owner via `owner.setAddress(...)`, so it's persisted and returned by `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\nBecause the owner's address is normalized *before* `householdMembers(...)` and `householdIdFor(...)` run, both the household duplicate-detection and the shared household id now compare the normalized form, as required. The existing `normalizeForHousehold` (case-insensitive, whitespace-collapsing) still applies consistently to both sides of each comparison.\n\nThe three new acceptance tests confirm the behavior: `\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`, `\"7 elm ave\"` \u2192 `\"7 ELM AVENUE\"`, and a whitespace-only address \u2192 `400`.",
